      "writtenOn": "2023-03-17T16:27:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah, so a `TEST_ASSERT` in `test_start_lid_close()` will cause `test_start_tablet_mode()` not to run? That\u0027s not at all what I\u0027d expect. I guess I can try confirming...\n\nOK, so I just tried this and I\u0027m convinced I\u0027m right, but feel free to tell me why I\u0027m wrong! :-) Here\u0027s what I tried:\n1. In `test_start_lid_close()`, I put a `TEST_ASSERT(false);` right before the `/* Going out o 360 mode, no change. */` comment. I ran `make -j run-tablet_broken_sensor` and I saw this at the end:\n```\ntest/tablet_broken_sensor.c:95: ASSERTION failed: false\nFail\nRunning test_start_tablet_mode...\n[4295.067307 Setting GPIO_LID_OPEN to 1]\n[4295.067308 Setting GPIO_TABLET_MODE_L to 0]\ntest/tablet_broken_sensor.c:118: ASSERTION failed: tablet_hook_count \u003d\u003d 2\nFail\nFail! (2 tests)\n```\n2. I moved the assert I added in step #1 to the _end_ of `test_start_lid_close()` (right before the `return EC_SUCCESS`). I ran `make -j run-tablet_broken_sensor` and I saw this at the end:\n```\ntest/tablet_broken_sensor.c:107: ASSERTION failed: false\nFail\nRunning test_start_tablet_mode...\n...\nOK\nFail! (1 tests)\n```\n\nThe above proves exactly what I was saying: a failure in the first test can cascade into a failure of the second test because you don\u0027t reset the state properly.\n\nSo I went and tried to fix it as I suggested, by making `test_init()` blank and moving the stuff to `before_test()`. I ended up getting it working but also finding a few surprising things. Specifically, I could avoid the cascading failures by making `before_test()` and `test_init()` look like this:\n\n```\nvoid before_test(void)\n{\n\t/* Make sure the device lid is in a consistent state (close). */\n\tgpio_set_level(GPIO_TABLET_MODE_L, 1);\n\tmsleep(50);\n\tgpio_set_level(GPIO_LID_OPEN, 0);\n\tmsleep(50);\n\n\ttablet_hook_count \u003d 1;\n}\n\nvoid test_init(void)\n{\n}\n```\n\nIf I do that then #1 and #2 above _both_ show just one failure (the assert(false)), not a cascading failure.\n\nThe surprising things I found while doing the above:\n* I could only avoid the cascading failures if I set \"tablet mode\" first and then \"lid open\". This is opposite the order you have. If I used the order you had then it wouldn\u0027t work. Is this a sign of a bug? It seems like maybe we ignore the \"lid closed\" GPIO if we\u0027re in \"tablet mode\" and then when \"tablet mode\" goes away we don\u0027t re-consider \"lid closed\"?\n* I needed the `msleep(50)` after the setting of the tablet mode. If I didn\u0027t have it then I also couldn\u0027t avoid the cascading failures. Even changing the `msleep()` after setting \"lid open\" to 100 wasn\u0027t enough. We absolutely had to let `GPIO_TABLET_MODE_L` debounce before setting `GPIO_LID_OPEN`. This feels like it must be a bug.",
      "parentUuid": "561e8929_02790b81",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 67,
